WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.285 --> 00:00:06.160
SHEKHAR BAPAT: Hello.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.530
I'm Shekhar Bapat.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.170
I'm the product manager
for Data Catalog

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.550
working for Google Cloud.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:15.240
And I'm here to talk about
Data Catalog for data discovery

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.980
and metadata management.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.560
I'm going to be joined by
Shruti Thaker from BlackRock

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.090
towards the end of
the presentation,

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.190
and she's going to share
their experience using Data

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.000
Catalog for BlackRock purposes.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.110
So what is Data Catalog?

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.290
Data Catalog,
simply put, enables

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.590
users to discover,
understand, and manage

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.790
all their data assets.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.110
So Data Catalog-- when
we released it in beta,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.470
we got a lot of feedback from
customers, where they said,

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.250
this is great.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.850
This is like Google Search
for my enterprise data assets.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.750
But they told us that that
data sets are not only in GCP,

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.090
but they are spread
across multiple silos.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.550
Some on them, some
on other clouds.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.340
And they wanted us to
help them discover data

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.620
across all of their data silos.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.930
They wanted one unified
single pane of glass.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.610
And that is what we
deliver here today,

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.040
an enterprise data catalog.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.660
But before I go into the details
of the Data Catalog service,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.420
I want to talk a little
bit about the genesis.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.450
Google feels this problem
of too many data assets

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.800
and how to discover
the valued data assets

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.740
way before other companies did.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.590
And so Google internally
built a data catalog,

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.410
and that data catalog is
a very scalable system.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.590
It indexes more than
100 million entries

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.610
and has been used actively by
tens of thousands of Googlers.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.010
Google also published
a paper on this.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.180
It's called the GOODS paper.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.400
We can easily find it.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.110
The GOODS paper
outlines a number

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.180
of techniques and technologies.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.670
So this is the backbone.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.200
The data catalog service
I'm going to talk about

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.900
is how their initiative
externalized the system

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.300
that we built for ourselves.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.040
If there is one
slide that captures

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.295
the essence of what Data Catalog
is today, it is this slide.

00:01:59.295 --> 00:02:01.170
So when we started to
build the Data Catalog,

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.540
we wanted to do something
different from what

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.220
the traditional catalogs do.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.890
The traditional
catalogs require users

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.759
to do a lot of heavy lifting.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.170
They require users to register
their data assets explicitly,

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.390
one by one.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.470
And the first thing we wanted to
do is to automate the process.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.050
The goal here is to
create a smart catalog.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.000
So Data Catalog automatically
syncs the technical metadata

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
from data sources like BigQuery,
Pub/Sub and Cloud Storage.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.840
So if you did a new
table in BigQuery,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.550
you don't need to
register anything.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.880
Within three seconds, that
table shows up in Data Catalog.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.730
So that's a very,
very useful feature

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.190
for a lot of our customers.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.250
The second thing that I
want to highlight here

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:45.100
is the auto-tagging of PII
data through our integration

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.380
with DLP.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.163
So our customers tell us that
they have thousands of tables,

00:02:49.163 --> 00:02:50.580
hundreds of columns
in each table,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.700
and often the column
names don't let on

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.310
as to whether it
has PII data or not.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.290
So DLP, as some
of you might know,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.360
stand for Data Loss Prevention.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.890
It's a service in GA.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.840
And we provide
integration with DLP

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.160
that enables you to use
DLP to scan your data.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:10.030
We can do a full scan of
the data, or to save money,

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.340
you can do sample scan.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.250
But DLP will identify the data.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:14.850
It puts semantics on the data.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.260
It says, ah, this is
not just any number.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.490
This can be a social security
number or a credit card number.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.960
And as it learned, it's able
to auto-detect PII data.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.680
And DLP works in multiple mode.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.730
In one mode, you can
take back the data.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.120
In another mode, you
can obfuscate the data.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.910
But often customers do not
want to change the data.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.460
They want to leave
the data as is,

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.470
but they want to
identify the data.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:40.640
And this integration with DLP
enables users do exactly that.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.890
DLP identifies the
data and creates

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:46.590
tags to specify that this
particular column is of type

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.460
PII, and is the type of PII.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.590
Now, lastly, we got a lot of
feedback from our customers

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:56.100
that they wanted to
get metadata from all

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.880
of their disparate data sources.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:02.160
So we modified our API to
enable users to provide metadata

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.630
from any source.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:04.710
But we didn't stop there.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.433
We also created open
source connectors,

00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:07.725
and we have put them in GitHub.

00:04:07.725 --> 00:04:09.600
It's accessible to everyone.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.340
Using those open
source connectors,

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.890
users like yourself can ingest
metadata from Hive, Oracle, SQL

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:20.829
Server, Teradata, Redshift,
MySQL, PostgreSQL--

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:22.160
the list continues to grow.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.660
We have also added [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.550
And as you can see, we can also
ingest with these connectors--

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.310
we can also ingest technical
metadata from BI tools

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.300
like Looker and Tableau.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.510
The end result is that
Data Catalog gives you

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.450
a unified view of
all your data assets.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.540
A single pane of glass--

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.548
you'll find them all.

00:04:43.548 --> 00:04:45.590
A little bit about the
Data Catalog architecture,

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:47.590
because one of the questions
that often comes up

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:49.592
is how robust is the service?

00:04:49.592 --> 00:04:50.675
How global is the service?

00:04:50.675 --> 00:04:52.590
How scalable is this service?

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.070
So we have launched
Data Catalog in GA.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.130
We did that at the end of April.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.250
We have launched the service in
23 different regions and market

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:02.200
agents.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:05.440
So it's a global service, and
it provides metadata residency

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:06.945
at rest.

00:05:06.945 --> 00:05:09.000
And there are many
building blocks.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.130
And the three big
building blocks

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:13.380
are real-time and
batch syncers that

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.150
bring metadata from
various sources

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.370
and put it in the
Data Catalog, then

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:21.247
the metadata store itself,
which stores all the metadata

00:05:21.247 --> 00:05:22.080
in the Data Catalog.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.900
So the metadata store is
just a transaction store.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.750
We use Spanner to build
the infrastructure.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.090
As you know, Spanner is
a globally distributed,

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.720
strongly consistent database.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:33.690
So we have a very
solid foundation.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:37.380
We also leverage the same
Google search technology that

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.270
follows Gmail and Google Drive.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:43.830
And that gives us search with
access control built into it.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.360
Gmail, as you know, has
more than a billion users,

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.960
and so we know that is a
very scalable technology.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:51.930
So Data Catalog is built on
this very strong foundation.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:55.260
In terms of innovation, what
we really introduce that is new

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.410
here is the concept
of structured tags.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:01.440
So the traditional catalogs
usually enable tags--

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.460
usually enable users
to put on tags.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:05.440
But those tags are
simple text strings.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.860
And as you'd assume,
it is very hard

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.700
to capture complex metadata.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:12.640
So we innovated, and we
said each tag actually

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:13.903
can be a schematized tag.

00:06:13.903 --> 00:06:15.820
But, you know, since
schema is taken for data,

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.910
we call them not
a schematized tag

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.465
but we call them
structured tags.

00:06:19.465 --> 00:06:22.090
So here is an example-- here is
an illustration of how the tags

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.650
look like in our catalog.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.470
Each tag has multiple
fields, and each field

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:27.880
is of a different type.

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:29.650
We support five different types.

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.980
We support Double, Boolean,
String, Enumerated,

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:34.960
and DateTime.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.060
And as a result, you
can create, for example,

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.820
the tags here in this
diagram on the left

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:43.390
are applied at the table level.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:46.460
The tags on the top are
applied at the column level.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.140
So our structured tags
are supported even

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.220
at the column level.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:52.810
And here is some
illustration of how

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:56.080
the tags can be used because
the user journeys that need

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:57.483
to be supported for example.

00:06:57.483 --> 00:06:58.900
You know, one of
the common things

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.990
that happens when analysts
come to work is they say, OK,

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:04.360
did last night's ETL
job run successfully?

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.500
Do I have fresh data?

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:10.900
And imagine if instead of, you
know, pinging data engineering

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:13.450
and asking them-- if
they could actually

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:16.830
put these structured tags in
the data asset themselves.

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:19.390
So in this illustration, I
show a data ingestion tag.

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:22.630
And analysts, by looking at
it, will know immediately

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:26.720
that the last night's ETL
job was completed normally.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.670
100,000 records were
processed, 100,000 were added,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.080
there were only
40 with warnings,

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.270
and there was zero errors.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:35.400
And they do not need actually,
you know, ping data engineering

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.345
and keep asking them
where it stands.

00:07:37.345 --> 00:07:40.220
Data governance can
create tags which

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:43.040
let users know what is the
classification of this data.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:43.850
Is it public?

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:44.510
Is it private?

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:45.650
Is it confidential?

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:46.830
Is it regulatory?

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:48.740
Where in the lifecycle
is this data?

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.740
Is it in test, in prod, in dev?

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:55.390
If there is a retention time
that is a constraint on this,

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:56.517
who is the business owner?

00:07:56.517 --> 00:07:58.100
What's the corresponding
on-prem data?

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.080
So there is a
whole bunch of what

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.660
we refer to as business metadata
that users need access to

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.265
and there is no easy
way of providing this.

00:08:06.265 --> 00:08:08.210
You know, people
often ask me, what's

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.830
the competition for
the Data Catalog.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:12.230
And the answer is
interestingly, you know,

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:17.530
Google Sheets, Excel sheets,
Wiki pages, Confluence pages.

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.000
Typically, the metadata is
spread all over the place,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.090
and there is no easy way to
make it available online,

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:24.650
make it searchable.

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:28.680
And our structured tags enable
users to do exactly that.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:30.970
I have another example
here for data quality.

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:33.617
Now often, one of the big
challenges analysts face

00:08:33.617 --> 00:08:35.159
is that they discover
the data asset,

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:37.149
but they have no
idea as to what's

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:38.210
the quality of this data.

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:39.409
Is it actually approved?

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:40.340
Is it usable?

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:43.070
Is it mostly null?

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:44.330
What is the cardinality?

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:45.920
What is the mean, max, median?

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:48.530
Data Catalog tags
enable you to compute

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:50.420
all the data quality
metrics and make

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:52.520
it available as business data.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:55.940
The PII tags are kind of
self-evident here, right?

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:59.570
So in this example, I have
two fields in the PII.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:02.030
One is the has_pii which
is of type Boolean,

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:03.470
and it's true here.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:05.420
The second is type_pii.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:08.350
For the second column, it
says, hey, it's an SSN.

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:09.970
For the fourth
column, it indicates

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:11.530
that it's an email address.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:15.080
So the structured tags provide
a very powerful, flexible,

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:18.560
and generic way to capture
all kinds of business metadata

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.510
and make it available
to your users.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:24.860
So let's talk a little bit
more about the structured tags.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:26.750
If there is a
structure to the tag,

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.160
there has to be a way to
specify the structure.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:30.830
So we have the
concept of templates.

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:33.630
We don't want to call it schema,
so we call them templates.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.830
So on the left, I show
an example of a template

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:37.400
where you can say, OK--

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.720
and by the way, all of this
is completely customizable.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:43.640
So if you as a user want
to create a tag template

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:47.557
and put 5 or 6 or 20 fields
in it, you can do that.

00:09:47.557 --> 00:09:49.140
So in this example,
I have this fields

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:50.780
called approved by
data governance,

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.570
and it is of type Boolean.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.290
And then I have
data classification,

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:55.720
which is of type Enumerated.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:59.150
And in this enumerated
type for my example,

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:02.540
I choose to define public,
sensitive, confidential,

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:06.040
and regulatory as
possible choices.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.880
Now, you as a user can
set your own choices.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:11.180
You can set your own
enumerated types, OK?

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:14.540
So there's a data lifecycle
that's also enumerated type.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.910
The delete by date, you know,
I mentioned a string here,

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.742
but actually it can be DateTime
now that we support DateTime.

00:10:20.742 --> 00:10:21.700
There is has_pii there.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:22.540
That it is Boolean.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:23.540
Those are the templates.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:26.600
Now on the right side, I
show an example of the tag

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:28.520
that we created
using this template.

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:30.230
So in this case,
approved by data

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.860
governance is set to
true data classification,

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:34.710
is set to sensitive, et cetera.

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:38.480
And as for the illustration,
once you have a template,

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:40.430
you can create tags
using that template

00:10:40.430 --> 00:10:41.780
in the mold of the template.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:44.678
And then you attach it to
individual data assets.

00:10:44.678 --> 00:10:46.220
So you can think of
this as some sort

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:48.230
of electronic sticky notes.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:51.120
Those are now applied
to every data asset.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.690
So whenever somebody
looks at a data asset,

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.990
they get to see all the
related metadata, right?

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:00.970
Now, how do people
discover the metadata?

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:02.970
So the first thing is,
you know, we have Google.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:04.970
We like to think we
know how to do search.

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:07.990
So we enable users to
do simple keyword search

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:10.220
so the business user can
simply type a keyword

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:11.940
and find the data asset.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:14.240
But the power
users among you who

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.890
actually want to do the
drill down [INAUDIBLE],,

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:19.630
or the needle in a
haystack way, for them,

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:23.160
we'll also enable facet search
where you can specifically say,

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.980
I want to limit my
search to this project

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:27.110
ID or these project IDs.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:30.350
I want to look at only BigQuery,
or I want to look at only views

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:32.780
where the column
matches a keyword,

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:36.970
or it has certain tags, the
tag, you know, has_pii is true,

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:38.390
or a combination thereof.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:42.410
You can create a more complex
query by combining with "and"

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:45.030
and "or," et cetera, and "not."

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.480
So you have the full power
of the search technology

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:48.598
at your disposal.

00:11:48.598 --> 00:11:50.430
OK?

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:52.070
One of the really
important things

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:53.930
that I want to
mention in our tagging

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:57.330
is that everything is
integrated with IAM.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:00.980
Security is built into Data
Catalog from the get-go.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.010
So we have two personas here.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.490
We have data governor persona.

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:07.190
And let's say in this
case, data governor has

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:10.270
full access to data assets
in project A, B, and C.

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:12.350
And we have another
person, our data analyst.

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:16.330
And data analyst has full access
to data assets in project A,

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:20.000
but metadata read only access
to data assets in project B,

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.220
and no access at
all to project C.

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:26.390
Even though all the metadata
resides in the search index,

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:28.470
when the data analyst
looks for data assets,

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:32.000
they see a different view
because in the 500 milliseconds

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:35.870
that we try to discern
something like 98% of the time,

00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:37.940
we've done probably
100,000 active checks

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:41.177
on access control checks to
figure out who can see what.

00:12:41.177 --> 00:12:43.010
And this is a really,
really important point

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:44.552
for many of our
customers, especially

00:12:44.552 --> 00:12:46.780
the financial customers,
banks, et cetera,

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:48.100
because security is paramount.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:49.520
And you cannot compromise that.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.570
And so as I mentioned,
we have it built into it.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:56.720
Now, building further on top
of that security framework,

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.575
the tags themselves are
also access controlled.

00:12:59.575 --> 00:13:01.490
All right, so in
this scenario, we

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.590
have data governor
persona who has

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:06.050
access to three different tags.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:08.080
And the data analyst
persona has access

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:10.370
to only two of those tags
because in many cases,

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:12.620
we have seen customers,
especially the data governance

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:15.860
team, create tags where they
put sensitive information

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:18.140
and they do not want
others to see this.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:20.650
And Data Catalog
enables you to do that.

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:24.830
So even the tags are also
under access control.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:28.615
Now, the next thing that I want
to mention is that, you know,

00:13:28.615 --> 00:13:30.320
we believe in a
very open system.

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:32.350
We believe that
it's your metadata.

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:34.370
You can do whatever
you want to do with it.

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:36.850
You want to bring in
metadata from other systems,

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:38.810
or you want to take
metadata data out,

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:40.330
we enable you to do that.

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:42.850
And in order for you
to do this in bulk,

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:45.980
as in bringing in large amounts
of metadata from outside

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:48.770
into our search index or
take out the metadata,

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.440
we enable APIs.

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:53.020
And we support seven
language libraries

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:55.750
so that you can
create all the action.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:58.610
Behind every UI,
there is an API call.

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:00.940
So everything is
available through the API.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:03.370
And many of our
customers actually

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:05.080
have their own front ends also.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:08.445
And they use the API
to power the backend

00:14:08.445 --> 00:14:10.570
because this is a much more
scalable and performing

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:12.450
backend.

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:19.440
And at this point, I would like
to invite Shruti from BlackRock

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:23.430
to share her experience using
Data Catalog, the reasons why

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:25.410
they choose to
partner with Google,

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:29.430
and how they achieve
their objectives.

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:31.500
SHRUTI THAKER: Hi, Shekhar, hi.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:32.355
I'm Shruti Thaker.

00:14:32.355 --> 00:14:34.490
I work in active
equities at BlackRock.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:37.160
And I'm head of vendor
data and alpha capture.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:39.760
I'm also the Google
administrator for our Google

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:43.250
Cloud instance in my team.

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:45.050
So why Data Catalog?

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:47.090
Well, when we
started our journey

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:50.360
bringing alternative
data into BlackRock

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:52.940
and making it available
for our investors to use

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:54.870
in their research and analysis.

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:57.170
We started to find that
the data that was coming in

00:14:57.170 --> 00:15:01.190
was very different to the more
traditional financial data that

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:05.720
already existed within BlackRock
within the data structures that

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:08.190
were made available to us.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:12.170
So what we were seeing were lots
of very dynamic schemas, lots

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:15.860
of different
formats, and working

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:20.150
with vendors that really didn't
connect with financial industry

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.420
as a rule.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:26.040
So the data was coming in
lots of different file types

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:27.840
as well.

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:32.110
On the other side, we
had a spectrum of users.

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:34.650
We had people both
geographically diverse,

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:39.750
from San Francisco to Hong
Kong, from Sydney to London,

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:42.060
as well as people who
had different levels

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:47.717
of comfortableness with
how to get to the data.

00:15:47.717 --> 00:15:50.050
We had people who were very,
very comfortable just going

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:53.010
into a server, going
to a CLI environment,

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:56.460
finding the file that they need,
pulling it, and analyzing it.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.620
On the other side,
we had lots of users

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:00.270
who really needed
that interface--

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:03.300
that front end which
allowed them to go in

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:09.030
and search for data
using keywords or tags,

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:14.310
and then be able to find that
data, and then work with it.

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:17.610
What we found was that we
really weren't seeing something

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:22.530
that could help us with both of
these issues within BlackRock.

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:25.980
And so we decided to go
and talk to our colleagues

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:29.070
at Google Cloud to see
if there was something

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:30.390
that they could help us with.

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:37.410
So we started talking
to Google Cloud in 2018,

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:40.590
and we explained to them
what we were trying to do.

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:43.380
And luckily for us
at that time, Shekhar

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:48.240
was just putting Data
Catalog into alpha.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:50.370
So we were able to
get in there and talk

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:52.530
to Shekhar and his
team and explain

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:54.120
what we were trying to do.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:58.080
And he showed us that it was
possible to find a solution

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:00.300
using Google Data Catalog.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:04.940
Because the Catalog
was in alpha when

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:09.589
we started working with Google
Cloud, it was possible for us

00:17:09.589 --> 00:17:13.609
to both help shape the product
and find a solution that

00:17:13.609 --> 00:17:15.770
would work for us in tandem.

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:18.140
One of the great things
about the Data Catalog

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:21.410
is that it's really well
integrated into the Google

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:22.680
Cloud ecosystem.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:28.640
So when you're looking at one
part of this puzzle, which

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.380
is the Data Catalog,
you're not then

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:33.710
having to build APIs
or build connections

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:36.770
into other parts of Google.

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:38.510
It's already
connected to BigQuery.

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:41.840
It's already connected to
storage and to Compute.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:45.730
So it was quite
easy for us to be

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:50.170
able to come up with an
integrated and complex front

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:54.400
end, which would really provide
our users a really powerful way

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:58.510
to find and analyze data.

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:02.920
We were also able to very
simply create templates,

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:04.900
both on the front
end to start with.

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:07.780
And then as our templates
became more complicated,

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:09.910
we worked with
Shekhar and the team

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:13.600
to put our templates into an
Excel file which we could then

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:15.970
upload directly via Python.

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:19.460
This actually made our
lives a lot easier.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:21.910
And we were able to create
tags as we wanted them.

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:25.390
We were able to say, OK, well,
we would like asset management

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:27.040
as a tag.

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:30.357
We would also like
different sectors as tags.

00:18:30.357 --> 00:18:31.690
And we were able to put them in.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:34.960
There wasn't anything
that was pre-created,

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:37.450
which really gave us the
freedom that we needed

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:41.110
to develop our own solution.

00:18:41.110 --> 00:18:47.440
As the Catalog has moved to
the general access phase,

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:49.660
new functionality
has been added,

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:53.890
including automatic
tagging and tagging

00:18:53.890 --> 00:18:55.840
of sensitive information.

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:59.350
We would like to start using
that in the next iteration

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:01.950
of our products.

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:04.740
Following is a short
demo of the product

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:09.140
that we built using Data
Catalog as our engine.

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.530
Hi, and welcome to Data Bodega.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:13.490
Data Bodega is an
application that

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:18.300
was created on GCP which
relies on the GCP Data Catalog

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:20.460
for its core functionality.

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:24.410
Data Bodega allows users to use
an intuitive and user-friendly

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:28.550
interface to try and find all of
the data sets available to them

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:33.790
and to do further simple
analysis where possible.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:36.430
When someone logs
into Data Bodega,

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:39.940
they'll be able to see all of
the tagged data sets split out

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:43.760
by category into
different sections.

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:48.690
Each one of the files in each
section consists of a data set.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:54.790
Users can also split by
sectors, regions, or style.

00:19:54.790 --> 00:19:57.510
The other way that people
can search the data

00:19:57.510 --> 00:20:01.940
is by looking at the intelligent
search on the left-hand side.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:03.660
Here they can enter a keyword.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:06.240
Or if they know the
table name or data set,

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:09.270
the names of those table
names or data sets.

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:11.910
Or they can do a filter search.

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:16.200
The filter search allows people
to use commonly thought of tags

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:19.310
and use those to do searches.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:21.350
For this demo, we're
going to do a search using

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:23.070
the intelligent search.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:25.290
I'm going to look for
some of this data.

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:27.920
This is data from the last
publicly available census

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:29.670
in the US.

00:20:29.670 --> 00:20:32.330
As you can see, we've got two
data sets, the postal codes

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:34.030
and the demographics.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:36.640
If we click on
geospatial_postalcodes,

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:39.540
it will open up the data set
and show me all of the tables

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:40.920
available there.

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:44.960
As you can see, there's only
a single data table available.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:47.970
If I click on that, on
the right-hand side,

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:51.560
you will notice that we now
see a preview of the table

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:54.230
as well as some information
that allows the user

00:20:54.230 --> 00:20:56.900
to understand how
fresh the data is

00:20:56.900 --> 00:21:02.090
and also if they need permission
to the data in GCP or BigQuery,

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:05.590
what project name to
ask for permission for.

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:07.790
You can see that we
can also show people

00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:12.180
the top 50 rows of each
data set that is available.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:14.930
This is refreshed
on a nightly basis.

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:16.730
And the only point
of this is to allow

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:19.100
people to have an
idea of what the data

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:21.630
set looks like, what kind
of numbers are involved,

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.880
et cetera.

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:28.350
You can also download this
data set as a CSV if needed.

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:32.050
The other tab available
here is the Analysis tab.

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:35.890
If you click on here, and the
data set has time series data,

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:39.840
there'll be some simple
graphing that can be possible.

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:43.030
You can also, however, open
the data directly in BigQuery

00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:46.140
if you have access
already in Tableau.

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:52.500
Or you can download the data
dictionary if that's available.

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:56.680
The way we created
this application is we

00:21:56.680 --> 00:22:03.100
went into Data Bodega project
in GCP and called it mosaic.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:08.510
And we went and created a
bunch of data tag templates.

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:10.480
Now the reason we
created so many

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:13.210
was that we needed
to have multiselect

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:18.890
on some of our tags, such
as region or country.

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:21.790
And the only way to do that was
to create a region or country

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:25.180
tag template and have
Boolean yes or no on there.

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:31.480
Fortunately we were
able to work with Google

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:36.370
to help us create the tag
templates in a format in Excel

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:41.510
which we were able to then just
upload into GCP using Python.

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.870
This made things a
lot easier and quicker

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:48.260
than creating the tag
templates on the front end.

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:51.920
We then went through all of
our data sets in BigQuery

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:55.730
and tagged all of them
using these tag templates.

00:22:55.730 --> 00:22:59.170
The next step is to start
auto-tagging data sets

00:22:59.170 --> 00:23:03.593
as they come in and they're
onboarded using various data

00:23:03.593 --> 00:23:05.260
attributes that are
made available to us

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:06.973
at the point of onboarding.

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:12.350
This is a quick
foray into what can

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:15.080
be built using Data Catalog.

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:17.180
We found it incredibly
useful to be

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:23.030
able to go in and connect and
allow users to go in and search

00:23:23.030 --> 00:23:25.520
for data without knowing
the names of the date set

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:28.610
or knowing particular
attributes of the data set

00:23:28.610 --> 00:23:33.010
and only knowing roughly the
topic that they're looking for.

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:34.680
We hope to work
further with Google

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:39.270
to create more functionality and
more depth to our product using

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:40.175
Data Catalog.

00:23:40.175 --> 00:23:41.550
SHEKHAR BAPAT:
Thank you, Shruti,

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:45.550
for that wonderful
presentation and the demo.

00:23:45.550 --> 00:23:49.000
So what are the next things
you can do as a user?

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:50.530
The first thing I
want to point out

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:53.410
is that you can launch
Data Catalog right away.

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:55.930
There is no setup required.

00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:59.450
Data Catalog indexes all the
metadata in the background

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:01.630
continuously, so
all you need to do

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:03.380
is to go to this
particular link,

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:06.020
the console.cloud.google.com.

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:07.750
Go to Data Catalog.

00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:10.120
You can select a project that
you wanted to start using.

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:13.720
Just enable the API,
and all the data assets

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:15.890
are already discoverable
to you, just

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:19.960
with a simple keyword search or
the powerful search language.

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:25.060
You can also use DLP to auto-tag
PII data in the Data Catalog.

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:29.680
The DLP integration is
available through the DLP UI.

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:32.230
It is also available
through the API.

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:35.110
And this link gives
you the details

00:24:35.110 --> 00:24:39.640
of how to do this to tag
PII data at the table level

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:42.070
as well as at column level.

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:45.790
And lastly, if you are
one of the users who

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:49.030
has data scattered in all
of these different silos,

00:24:49.030 --> 00:24:50.560
and you're finding
it really, really

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:53.600
hard to discover
those data assets,

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:56.590
you can use one
of our connectors.

00:24:56.590 --> 00:24:59.680
And all the connectors are
listed in the Google's GitHub

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:00.790
repo.

